      // [src_yifter_aw, src_yifter_ramsak, src_yifter_johnson, src_yifter_wa2004 | 1979-1980 | athletics press + federation tribute + rival's column + interview] Athletics Weekly and the IAAF tribute both report that he practised launching his sprint with 300 m to go and watched rivals until five laps remained; Len Johnson, a runner who watched and raced him, says he held the kick until the last 300 m in both Moscow finals and used a similar sit-and-sprint pattern in the 1979 World Cup 10,000 m. A rehearsed race plan is documented in outline, but the account of the rehearsal is his own (relayed by two federation writers) and the other sources retell the same finishing pattern, so it is one behaviour seen in several outcomes.
